<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 1st Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.3)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Colors of Things that Go</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> That's a Blue Bus.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Catch the main idea of a short, simple story or announcement with visual support or illustrations. Interactive: Identify objects in the immediate environment in response to simple questions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can recognize colors and transportation-related words, such as "car" and "bus," in simple announcements with visual aids (e.g., circling the picture of a "red car" when it is mentioned in a short visual announcement). Can follow simple instructions related to transportation items as heard in simple stories (e.g., "Find the green car."). Can respond to simple questions about transportation vehicles by pointing or naming them (e.g., "What color is the car?").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 20px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students with a lively "Hello, everyone! Let's sing a song about things that go!" and leads a short "Wheels on the Bus" song with actions (TPR). The teacher then shows flashcards of a blue bus, red car, and green bike, asking students to point and listen as the teacher names them.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a flashcard of a blue bus. Teacher says clearly: "This is a bus. It is blue. A blue bus." (pointing to the bus and the color). Teacher emphasizes the /b/ sound in "bus." Teacher repeats with a red car and green bike, highlighting the /k/ sound in "car" and the /g/ sound in "green" and the /b/ sound in "bike." Teacher uses gestures to indicate "this" and "that."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher uses simple questions and gestures to check understanding.
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher shows a flashcard of a bus: "Is this a car or a bus?" (Teacher points to options if needed).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher shows a red car flashcard: "What color is the car?" (Teacher encourages one-word responses or pointing).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to a blue bus: "Is this a red bus?" (Students shake heads or say "No").</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Colors of Things that Go."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short, simple audio announcement or story with accompanying large, colorful picture cards or digital slides showing different colored vehicles (car, bus, bike, truck, train, taxi, red, blue, green, yellow, orange). The audio describes each vehicle's color and type.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Listening: Receptive: Catch the main idea of a short, simple story or announcement with visual support or illustrations.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher introduces the activity: "Now, let's listen to a story about colors and vehicles! Listen carefully and look at the pictures."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher plays a pre-recorded audio (e.g., "Hello, children! Look! It is a red car. See? That's a blue bus. This is a green bike. And there's a yellow truck!"). Each sentence is accompanied by the corresponding image on a screen or flashcard shown by the teacher.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Comprehension tasks (Gist/Identification):</b>
                            <ul>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">After the first listen, the teacher asks: "What did we hear about? Colors or animals?" (Gist: Colors).</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher plays the audio again, pausing after each description: "It is a red car." "Point to the red car!" (Students point to the correct picture). "That's a blue bus." "Point to the blue bus!" (Reinforcing 'blue' and 'bus' and the 'that' demonstrative).</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher then holds up color flashcards: "Listen: 'yellow truck.' What color is it?" (Students hold up the yellow flashcard). Emphasize /y/ in "yellow."</li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar:</b> Color + noun (e.g., "green car"), Present simple (e.g., "It is a red bike.").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> car, bus, bike, truck, train, taxi; green, blue, red, yellow, purple, orange.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> /k/ in "car", /b/ in "bus", /y/ in "yellow."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pragmatic:</b> Describing objects (e.g., "It's a blue bus."), Answering questions (pointing/gestures).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Sociolinguistic:</b> Norms of politeness for making requests (e.g., "Please, point to the blue car.").</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ol>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice: "Listen and Circle" (Worksheet):</b> Students receive a worksheet with various uncolored vehicles (e.g., 2 cars, 2 buses, 2 bikes) and a crayon for each color. The teacher gives instructions: "Listen carefully! Circle the blue bus." (Students find and circle the bus). "Circle the red car." "Circle the yellow bike." (Focus on specific colors and vehicle nouns, incorporating /b/, /k/, /y/ sounds).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice: "Listen and Color/Point":</b> The teacher holds up large picture cards of vehicles. "It is a green car. Please, color the car green." (Students color their worksheet pictures accordingly). "That is an orange taxi. Please, point to the orange taxi on your paper." (Teacher uses "Please" for politeness). The teacher provides instructions such as "Color the train blue," "Color the truck red," "Color the bike yellow."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Group Interaction / Peer Check: "Listen and Show":</b> Students work in small groups with sets of mini-flashcards (pictures of vehicles in different colors). The teacher says: "Show me a purple bike!" (Students quickly find and hold up the card). "Show me a big red truck!" "Show me a small green car!" (Teacher observes group members helping each other identify the correct card based on listening, fostering peer checking. The teacher also asks individual students, "What color is this bike?" for one-word answers).</li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-17 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Colors of Things that Go."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format: "Traffic Controller Game" (Guided TPR Activity).</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher lays out a large floor map or draws a simple road/city scene on the whiteboard. Students are given cut-outs of different colored vehicles (car, bus, bike, truck, train, taxi).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher explains: "You are traffic controllers! Listen to my instructions and place the vehicles on the road."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher gives sequential instructions, focusing on colors and vehicles: "Please, put the blue bus on the road." (Students find the blue bus and place it). "Next, put the red car near the blue bus." "Move the green truck to the stop sign." "Where is the yellow taxi? Please, drive the yellow taxi to the station."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher observes students' ability to follow multi-step instructions and correctly identify the specific vehicle-color combination. Teacher can also ask "What color is that train?" to elicit short verbal responses like "Purple!" or pointing.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">This task directly connects to the theme "That's a Blue Bus" and scenario "Colors of Things that Go" by having students interact with colored vehicles based on spoken input.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy: Observation Checklist.</b> The teacher uses a simple checklist to note individual student's ability to identify and respond to specific color-vehicle combinations.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task: "Listen and Follow Instructions."</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students have a pre-printed worksheet with a grid of various colored vehicles.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher gives 3-4 simple, distinct instructions aloud:
                            <ol>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Point to the green car."</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Circle the blue bus."</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Put an 'X' on the yellow bike."</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What color is this train?" (Teacher points to a purple train on the worksheet; students point or say "Purple!").</li>
                            </ol>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accuracy in identifying (pointing/circling/marking) the correct color + vehicle combination from spoken instruction.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to follow simple 1-step instructions.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible (even if one-word) responses to "What color...?" questions.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Thumbs up if you can hear 'red car' and point to it!" (Students show thumbs up or down).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Point to the color word you liked hearing today!" (Teacher shows color words).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Student and teacher reflection.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new colors or vehicles did you hear today?" (Teacher elicits responses like "Blue!" "Bus!")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Was it easy or a little hard to listen and find the blue bus?" (Students can show with hand gestures - flat hand for easy, wavy hand for a little hard).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well today? The TPR activities and the visual aids were highly engaging for 1st graders. Students responded well to simple, repeated instructions and the "Listen and Show" game. Phonemic awareness practice for /k/ and /b/ was integrated naturally.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for next lesson? Some students still confused "car" and "train" by sound alone. More distinct pronunciation practice and visual differentiation will be needed. For next lesson, I will provide more controlled listening for specific sounds. I also need to ensure consistent use of "this" vs "that" in modeling.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence: In Lesson 2 (Reading Comprehension), students will see these same colors and vehicle words written down. We will practice recognizing the written form of "blue bus" and "red car" after hearing them today.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">2-3 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>